  "hints": [
    "Run the simulation. The SQL database is overloaded, but look at what kind of traffic it gets: reads or writes?",
    "SQL read replicas copy data from one primary. Every write still lands on that single primary, so replicas add zero write capacity.",
    "Swap the SQL database for a store whose replicas are shards that split writes: NoSQL with 2 replicas, or a Time-Series DB with 2 replicas. A queue in front of SQL only builds a backlog."
  ],
  "scoring": {
    "weights": {
      "performance": 0.35,
      "reliability": 0.3,
      "cost": 0.2,
      "architecture": 0.15
    },
    "latencyTargetMs": 50,
    "costBudget": 900
  },
  "explanation": [
    "Single-leader SQL scales reads with replicas, but writes are bounded by one machine. For write-heavy workloads you need partitioning, where each shard owns a slice of the keys and takes its share of writes.",
    "Sensor data is a textbook fit for a time-series or wide-column store: append-only, keyed by device and time, read back in ranges.",
    "A queue smooths bursts, but it can't fix a sustained rate higher than the consumer can absorb. The backlog just grows forever.",
    "Retire the replicas you no longer need. They were costing $600 a month and doing nothing for this workload."
  ]
}
